-- title:
--   Convergence of Fontaine's w-series at nilpotent points
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring, $p$ a prime, $d$ a natural number, and let $a\colon \mathbb N \to \mathcal O[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]$ be a sequence of formal power series in $d$ variables over $\mathcal O$ each of which has vanishing constant coefficient. Let $g$ be a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra which is adically complete for the ideal $J=(p)$ generated by the image of $p$, and let $x\colon \mathrm{Fin}\,d \to g$ be a point all of whose coordinates lie in the radical of $J$. Write $b_n = a_n(x) \in g$ for the $J$-adic evaluation $\mathrm{eval}_2$ of $a_n$ along $\mathcal O \to g$ at $x$ (the coefficient ring carrying the discrete topology and $g$ the $J$-adic one). The assertion is that in the localisation $g[1/p]$ of $g$ away from $p$, in which the image of $p$ is a unit, the partial sums $S_N = \sum_{n<N} p^{-n}\, b_n^{\,p^n}$ converge $p$-adically to the element $\mathrm{wSeries}$ attached to $(b_n)_n$, that is: for every $s$ there is $n_0$ with $S_N - \mathrm{wSeries} \in p^s g$ (the $g$-submodule of $g[1/p]$ spanned by $p^s$) for all $N \ge n_0$. Since $\mathrm{wSeries}$ is defined as a choice of such a limit when one exists and as $0$ otherwise, the content is that a $p$-adic limit of the partial sums does exist.
--
--   This is the convergence statement underlying Fontaine's map $w$ on a formal group over a $p$-adically complete base: at a point with topologically nilpotent coordinates the divided series $\sum_n p^{-n} a_n(x)^{p^n}$ has an honest $p$-adic limit, so that $\mathrm{wSeries}$ evaluates to that limit rather than to the default value $0$. It is used in the comparison of $w$-values at nearby points and in the bijectivity statements for the Fontaine functor on Honda systems with split coordinates.
